  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued Issued by National Weather Service Glasgow MT/ \nIssued Issued by National Weather Service Glasgow MT/ \n\nMet overview:\n\nBroad upper level troughing continues across the western US, \nwith a reinforcing lobe of vorticity/compact upper low diving \nsouth/southeastward through AB and SK toward NE MT this morning. \nAt least patchy clearing has allowed for at least patchy dense \nfog and areas of low clouds to develop early this morning, \nprimarily across the plains. The fog looks to slowly diminish \nthrough the day today as daytime heating and light northwest \nwinds associated with a cold front begin to mix the surface \nout ever so slightly. Meanwhile, areas near the ID border east \nof Monida Pass are just close enough to the broad upper level \ntroughing that areas of largely light snow will continue through \nthe morning and through the day today. Accumulations in this \narea look to be largely light, though areas near West \nYellowstone will see a few inches of snow through the day today. \n\nAs the aforementioned upper system approaches this afternoon \nand evening, light snow begins to spread over much of the \nregion. Ahead of the area of light widespread snow, there looks \nto be marginal, yet sufficient low-level instability to result \nin a few snow showers, mainly across Central and SW MT. The more \nwidespread snow that develops through the afternoon and evening \nlargely looks light, though isolated instances of a few inches \nnear terrain in northwesterly upslope areas is forecast. Given \ncolder temperatures in place now, this lighter snow will have \nbetter success accumulating on roadways. This snow largely \ndiminishes into Saturday morning, with areas in northwesterly \nfavored areas seeing continued light snow through the afternoon \nSaturday. \n\nTransient ridging briefly builds in Sunday ahead of a much \nlower confidence forecast heading into next week. Several upper \nlevel disturbances off the west coast will be in place to open \nnext week. Whether they move east across the Pacific NW or \nshift southward before moving east toward the Northern Rockies \nMonday remains to be seen. After the uncertainty to open the \nweek, around 80% of cluster guidance members favor some form \nof at least weak upper level ridging and associated warmer than \naverage temperatures mid-week toward Thanksgiving. -AM\n\nUncertainty:\n\nFog this morning: A mix of very low clouds and at least patchy \ndense fog will continue across the plains early this morning. \nAt this time webcams are not suggesting the fog is widespread, \nhence we are holding off on a dense fog advisory for the time \nbeing. Should the fog become more widespread in any area, a Dense \nFog Advisory will be considered. \n\nSnow today and tonight: In most areas the snow looks to be \nlargely light. The primary exception is the areas around West \nYellowstone, which look to pick up a few inches of snow today. \nGiven this is not a particularly notable amount for this area, \nand that much lower totals are forecast elsewhere in this \nforecast zone, we will continue to hold off on a Winter Weather \nAdvisory. -AM",
